FEX on Android: faccessat2 without the faccessat2 syscall.

Android blocks faccessat2. Guest faccessat2 is computed as glibc 2.41's faccessat() does without
it: unknown flags are EINVAL; flags 0 (or AT_EACCESS with equal real and effective ids) use the
faccessat syscall; otherwise fstatat, then the faccessat syscall again when the file is no symlink,
the path is not empty and the real ids are the ones to check, so read-only and noexec mounts, ACLs
and SELinux count. Only in the remaining cases do the permission bits decide, with the super-user
rule for X_OK. AT_EMPTY_PATH is passed to fstatat, as the kernel's faccessat2 accepts it.
FileManager::Access uses the faccessat syscall instead of glibc's faccessat(), which tries
faccessat2 first.

